// The explanations of the codes of 0.26.2's text and syntax: `where` and
// argument-position `impl Trait` (front/parser/items/params.vl),
// visibility (`pub(crate)`, front/modules), format literals
// (front/check/format.vl); docs/grammar.md, "Items", "Modules",
// "Formatting". (0.26.3) f-strings removed: syntax.fstring
// (front/parser/exprs.vl); `#[deprecated]` (front/check/deprecated.vl).

use crate::tools::explain::Explain;

pub const TEXT_COUNT: i64 = 11;

pub static TEXT_TABLE: [Explain; 11] = [
    Explain {
        code: "where.unsupported", severity: "error",
        means: "(0.26.2) A `where` predicate whose left side is not a type parameter of the item: `where I::Item: Display`, `where Vec<T>: Debug`. In 0.26 `where` is another way to write a parameter's bounds, `fn f<T>(x: T) where T: Display` is `fn f<T: Display>(x: T)`; a bound on any other type comes with the rust edition. The predicate is left out.",
        fix: "Bound a parameter instead: for an iterator's items, a parameter for the item type, `I: Iterator<Item = X>, X: Display`.",
        example: "error[where.unsupported] 'I::Item: Display': a 'where' bound is on a type parameter of the item, and 'I::Item' is not one | help: a bound on a type that is not a parameter comes with the rust edition; bound a parameter instead: 'I: Iterator<Item = X>, X: Display'",
    },
    Explain {
        code: "type.impl-position", severity: "error",
        means: "(0.26.2) `impl Trait` outside a function's parameter: in a result (`-> impl Display`), a field, a `let`, a type alias. A parameter's `x: impl Display` is a hidden type parameter of the function, `fn f<T: Display>(x: T)`, that each call infers; a result's `impl Trait` (a type the function picks and hides) comes with the rust edition.",
        fix: "Name a type parameter, or write the type itself: `fn f() -> i64`; for a closure, a result of a named type (`Vec`, a struct) or a parameter the caller passes.",
        example: "error[type.impl-position] 'impl Display' is a parameter's type only, in this release | help: name a type parameter instead, 'fn f<T: Display>() -> T'; 'impl Trait' in a result, a field or a 'let' comes with the rust edition",
    },
    Explain {
        code: "item.private", severity: "error",
        means: "(0.26.2) An item (a function, a type, a constant, a static, a module) used outside the module that declares it, where it is not visible (Rust's E0603): an item not marked `pub` is private to its module and the modules inside it; one marked `pub(crate)`, `pub(super)` or `pub(in path)` is visible only up to that module (the message says which: `crate 'std'`, `module 'a::b'`). Until 0.26.2 this was a `type error`.",
        fix: "In your own program, mark it `pub` where it is declared, or widen its restriction (the help gives the declaration; `volec fix --only pub` writes `pub` for a private one and `pub(crate)` for a restricted one). Std's `pub(crate)` items are std's own: use its public API.",
        example: "error[item.private] 'super_fn' is visible only in module 'outer' | help: make it visible in its crate: `pub(crate) fn super_fn` at main.vl:3:20",
    },
    Explain {
        code: "vis.path", severity: "error",
        means: "(0.26.2) `pub(in path)` whose path names no module, or a module that is not the item's own or one around it (Rust's E0742); or `pub(super)` at a crate's root, which has no parent in the crate. The path starts at `crate`, `self`, `super` (in std, `std::` or `super::`). The item is then visible in its crate.",
        fix: "Name the item's module or one of its ancestors: `pub(in crate::a)` for an item of `a::b`, or write `pub(super)` or `pub(crate)`.",
        example: "error[vis.path] 'pub(in crate::outer::b)': the path names a module that is not around this item | help: 'pub(in path)' names the item's module or one around it: 'pub(in crate::a)', 'pub(super)', 'pub(crate)'",
    },
    Explain {
        code: "format.syntax", severity: "error",
        means: "(0.26.2) A format literal (a plain string literal that is the first argument of println, print, eprintln, eprint, format_in, format_args, push_fmt, Fmt::write, panic, the message of assert and debug_assert, or a contract's `else`) whose braces do not make holes: a `{` never closed, a `}` with no `{`. Its grammar is Rust's std::fmt: `{}`, `{0}`, `{name}`, each with an optional `:spec`, and `{{` and `}}` for a brace of the text. Before 0.26.2 a plain literal was printed as it is, braces and all (`warning[format]` said so).",
        fix: "Double the braces of the text: `println(\"{{\")` prints `{` (`volec fix --suggested` writes it; `volec fix --only fstring` doubles them in a whole program).",
        example: "error[format.syntax] unmatched '}' in a format string | help: a '}' of the text is written '}}'",
    },
    Explain {
        code: "format.spec", severity: "error",
        means: "(0.26.2) A hole's spec, after its `:`, that is not Rust's: `[[fill]align][sign]['#']['0'][width]['.' precision][type]`, align `<` `^` `>`, sign `+`, width and precision an integer, `N$` or `name$` (precision also `*`), type `?` `x?` `X?` `x` `X` `o` `b` `e` `E` `p`, and vole's `c` (a u8 as its byte). `{:#?}`, Rust's Debug over several lines, is not in this release.",
        fix: "Write the spec in Rust's order: `{x:>8.2}`, `{n:#010x}`, `{s:*^9}`, `{v:?}`; for `{:#?}`, `{:?}`.",
        example: "error[format.spec] invalid format spec ':q': unknown format trait 'q' | help: a spec is [[fill]align][+][#][0][width][.precision][type]: align < ^ >, width and precision N, N$ or name$ (precision also *), type ? x? X? x X o b e E p c: '{x:>8.2}', '{v:?}', '{n:#010x}', '{s:*^9}'",
    },
    Explain {
        code: "format.args", severity: "error",
        means: "(0.26.2) A format call's arguments do not match its literal's holes (Rust's \"argument never used\", \"invalid reference\"): an argument (positional or named, `name = expr`) that no hole takes, a hole (`{}`, `{N}`, `N$`, `.*`) with no argument for it; or arguments after a first argument that is not a literal (`println(s, x)`): a format string is a literal, a `const` or a variable is printed as a value.",
        fix: "Give each hole its argument, after the literal and in order, or remove the argument; for a variable format, format the pieces apart (`print(s); println(\"{}\", x)`) or build the value with `format_args(\"..\", x)`.",
        example: "error[format.args] this argument is never used: no hole of the format string takes it | help: write a hole for it, '{}', or remove it",
    },
    Explain {
        code: "format.expr-hole", severity: "error",
        means: "(0.26.2) A hole of a format literal holding an expression, `{p.a}`, `{x + 1}`, `{v.len()}`: a hole names a local, a parameter, a `const` or a `static` (Rust 2021's inline captures), or takes the next argument. (An f-string's hole, `f\"{p.a}\"`, held any expression; f-strings are removed in 0.26.3, `syntax.fstring`.)",
        fix: "Write `{}` and pass the expression after the literal: `println(\"{}\", p.a)` (`volec fix --suggested` moves it).",
        example: "error[format.expr-hole] '{p.a}': a hole of a format string holds a name, not an expression | help: write '{}' and pass the expression after the format string, as Rust does: 'println(\"{} {x}\", a.b)'",
    },
    Explain {
        code: "syntax.defer", severity: "error (from 0.26.2)",
        means: "`defer expr;` or `defer { .. }`, which ran its statement when the block exited (0.6 to 0.26.1), is removed: Rust has no `defer`. What it did is a guard, a value whose `Drop` does it: drops run at the end of the scope, last first, on every way out (`return`, `?`, `break`), as defers did. `defer` stays a keyword so that this error names it.",
        fix: "Wrap the resource in a guard: a struct holding it, with `impl Drop` doing the cleanup, made where the defer stood (`let _closer = Closer { fd };` lives to the end of the block; a bare `_` drops at once). A `Vec`, `Buf` or `HashMap` frees itself: `defer v.free();` needs nothing.",
        example: "extern \"C\" { fn close(fd: i32) -> i32; }\n\
            \n\
            struct Closer { fd: i32 }\n\
            \n\
            impl Drop for Closer {\n\
            \x20   fn drop(inout self) { unsafe { close(self.fd); } }\n\
            }\n\
            \n\
            fn use_fd(fd: i32) {\n\
            \x20   let _closer = Closer { fd };   // was: defer close(fd);\n\
            \x20   // .. fd is closed when the function returns, however it returns\n\
            }\n\
            \n\
            main.vl:9:5: error[syntax.defer] 'defer' is removed (0.26.2): Rust has none | help: a guard whose Drop does it",
    },
    Explain {
        code: "syntax.fstring", severity: "error (from 0.26.3)",
        means: "An f-string, `f\"x = {x}, len {v.len()}\"` (0.15 to 0.26.2), is removed: a format string is Rust's, a plain literal that is the first argument of println, print, eprintln, format_in, panic, push_fmt, Fmt::write, a contract's `else` or format_args (0.26.2). A hole names a local, a parameter, a const or a static, or takes the next argument, `{}`; an expression is an argument after the literal. The error comes once per file, at its first f-string, with how many the file has.",
        fix: "`volec fix --only fstring --keep-braces` rewrites every f-string of the program, once: a name hole stays, another hole becomes `{}` (`{:spec}`) and its expression an argument after the literal, in order; an f-string that is no format function's argument becomes `format_args(\"..\", args)`. Keep `--keep-braces` for code written under 0.26.2, where a plain literal at a format position is a format string already: `println(\"{n} items\")` prints `3 items`, and without the flag the fix would double its braces to keep printing `{n} items`. Drop it only for code written before 0.26.2, where a plain literal's braces were text: `println(\"{x}\")` printed `{x}`; without the flag the fix writes `println(\"{{x}}\")`, which still does, where with it the literal would print x's value.",
        example: "fn main() {\n\
            \x20   let v = [1, 2, 3];\n\
            \x20   let n = 3;\n\
            \x20   println(f\"n = {n}, sum {v[0] + v[1]}\");   // now: println(\"n = {n}, sum {}\", v[0] + v[1]);\n\
            }\n\
            \n\
            main.vl:4:13: error[syntax.fstring] f-strings are removed (0.26.3): a format string takes the values as arguments | help: a name stays in its hole and an expression goes after the literal: `println(\"{n} {}\", v.len())`; elsewhere `format_args(\"..\", args)` | try: volec fix --only fstring --keep-braces main.vl",
    },
    Explain {
        code: "item.deprecated", severity: "warning",
        means: "(0.26.3) A use of an item marked `#[deprecated]` (Rust's attribute: `#[deprecated]`, `#[deprecated = \"note\"]`, `#[deprecated(since = \"1.2\", note = \"use new_fn\")]`), on a function, a method, a struct, an enum, a trait, a type alias, a constant, a static or a field: a call, a name, a type, a path, a `use`, a field read or a struct literal's field. The message gives the item's note; the help, where it is marked. A use inside a deprecated item (a recursive call, a derived impl, a deprecated method reading a deprecated field) is not one, as in Rust, and std's uses of its own deprecated items are std's.",
        fix: "Do what the note says (it names what to use instead); for std's `Buf`, `volec fix --only buf` rewrites each binding to `String<A>` (text) or `Vec<u8, A>` (bytes).",
        example: "warning[item.deprecated] use of deprecated function 'old_fn': use new_fn | help: marked #[deprecated] at main.vl:2:5",
    },
];
